          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "**If you have any of these, seek emergency care now:** Vomiting blood, vomit that looks like coffee grounds, or black tarry stools need emergency care now. So does any bleeding with dizziness, faintness, collapse, confusion or breathlessness (Source: Gastrointestinal bleeding). Heartburn-like discomfort, breathlessness, unusual tiredness, or discomfort in the jaw, back or upper abdomen can be signs of a heart attack. A history of reflux does not rule this out, and antacid relief does not either. If chest or related symptoms appear, seek emergency care (Source: Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack).\n\n**Features that should prompt investigation rather than a routine trial of acid-reducing medicine:** The GERD guideline summary \"GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it\" lists these: difficulty swallowing or pain on swallowing; unintentional weight loss; gastrointestinal bleeding; iron-deficiency anaemia; persistent vomiting; a palpable mass or a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer; and new symptoms at an older age. The guidance calls for prompt assessment, usually endoscopy, but gives no set time frame, so ask a clinician how soon. For the age item, it gives no numeric cutoff.\n\n**If symptoms do not respond to treatment:** The same guideline says to check adherence and timing with meals first, then refer for endoscopy and, in selected people, pH testing. It also notes that many people who do not respond have a different condition, which more acid suppression will not treat.\n\n**Limits of these sources:** The sources I reviewed found no disagreement between authorities on these alarm features. They also do not state how strong the evidence is for each feature, so I cannot say which ones rest on stronger data. These passages do not apply to any particular person. If you have one of these features, contact a clinician promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
